From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1vivy <1vivy@tutanota.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 18:50:30 -0600
Subject: feat(web): pick through the file system access dialog and remember
 handles

`pickFile()`/`pickFiles()` only ever went through `<input type="file">`, so a
picked file had no handle: it could not be remembered across a page reload, and
its content could only be read through a `blob:` URL. The save dialog has
already been behind `FilePickerWebOptions.saveMode` since 4.2.0, so the open
dialog was the missing half of the same feature (#770).

Add `FilePickerWebOptions.dialog`, `pickerId` and `types`:

- `input` (default) keeps today's behaviour, so no existing app changes.
- `auto` uses `showOpenFilePicker()` where the browser has it and the pick can
  be filtered with `types`, and the input everywhere else, including where
  `type`/`allowedExtensions` filter the pick, since the dialog has no wildcard
  support and would otherwise accept every file.
- `fileSystemAccess` always uses the dialog, throwing `UnsupportedError` or
  `ArgumentError` when it cannot.

`WebPlatformFile` keeps the browser `File`, so ranged reads go through
`file.slice()`, and the `FileSystemFileHandle`. `persist()` stores the handle in
IndexedDB and returns a token, `restore()`/`forget()` read and drop it, and
`hasAccess()`/`requestAccess()` report and renew the read permission the
browser drops between sessions. A file restored without access still carries its
name and handle, so an app can offer to renew instead of picking again.

The token store is adapted from dir_picker's web_tokens.dart (MIT), with its
notice kept in the file header. All of the interop is `dart:js_interop` and
`package:web`, so it compiles for Wasm as well.

Rebased from bf434d23 onto 7b8aec33 (file_picker 13.4.0; file_picker_web is
unchanged since 4.2.1). The first version conflicted in
lib/src/file_picker_web.dart with the picker-session lifecycle added since
(`_activeSession`, stale-session cancellation, #2324). The resolution keeps
that supersede/cancel logic untouched and takes the file system access branch
before any `<input>` session is created: a dialog pick returns before the
input session exists.

Checked together with the next commit, which builds on this API; see there.

